The Samurai's ranks seems to be based on a success story in the Sengoku Era 
(the Warring State Period, the era of warlords). A young man takes a sword, 
entries to some Daimyo's army as an ashigaru, earns his territory for rewards 
of his achievement in war, organize his own army and becomes a mightier daimyo. 
Finally he unite all states in Japan and is crowned the title "Shogun" from 
the Tennou(emperor). So the story from a ronin to the Shogun is familiar to 
Japanese. 

However, the title "Hatamoto" in these ranks is quite strange. Hatamoto is 
the rank which was created in the Edo Era, after Tokugawa Ieyasu united 
Japan and became to the Shogun. Hatamoto is originally created as an army 
which is under the direct command of the Shogun, but in reality 
almost of them are just government official with low salary. 
(even some of Hatamoto had no job at all...) 
Some of them lost their morality from their high pride, becomes to a gang, 
behaves outrageously and even kills townsfolk just for pleasure.
So I feel that Hatamoto does not fit Samurai's ranks.

